using a defibrillator. I am also capturing key details such as the major causes of cardiac arrest.\n\n\n**Defining Hemorrhage**\n\nI'm now extracting information related to hemorrhage. I am zeroing in on definitions, specifically, the escape of whole blood, including from blood vessels. I'm focusing on \"Hemorrhage\" and defining it within this context. I am also planning on extracting other information in the Hemorrhage section.\n\n\n**Defining Medical Scenarios**\n\nI'm now extracting and organizing details from the document, and I have extracted information on Septicemia and Basic Life Support. I've successfully pulled the definitions and common clinical features of septicemia, and the definition of basic life support.\n\n\n**Defining Life Support**\n\nI've extracted crucial details regarding Advanced Cardiac Life Support (ACLS). I now have the main components, including early recognition, high-quality CPR, and defibrillation. Furthermore, I have the definitions of CPR, mechanical ventilation, and their primary purposes. The focus remains on efficiently cataloging key concepts for practical application.\n\n\n**Defining Diagnostic Measures**\n\nI am now extracting and organizing information, and have successfully defined Arterial Blood Gas (ABG) Analysis and Electrocardiogram (ECG). I have captured the definition of the ABG test, including its clinical significance. For the ECG, I've detailed its definition, as well as the major waves and what each wave represents.\n\n\n**Defining Oxygen Therapy**\n\nI'm now extracting definitions and key features for specific medical conditions and treatments, specifically focusing on \"Oxygen Therapy,\" \"Psoriasis,\" \"Eczema Dermatitis,\" and \"Urticaria Hives.\" I have gathered definitions, common indications, characteristic clinical features, and common types for each, ensuring I organize each element for straightforward review.\n\n\n**Defining Skin Conditions**\n\nI'm now extracting information for several skin conditions. I've focused on \"Urticaria Hives,\" pulling the clinical features and triggers associated with the condition. I've also defined Acne Vulgaris, its common types, and I am gathering key definitions for related skin disorders. I've successfully collected characteristic features of vitiligo and the defining characteristics.\n\n\n\n**Defining Infectious Diseases**\n\nI am now extracting and organizing information, and I've successfully collected the defining characteristics for \"Leprosy (Hansen's Disease)\", and \"Protein-Energy Malnutrition (PEM)\", including their core definitions, and specific neurological complications associated with Leprosy. I am prioritizing key details to create concise data points.\n\n\n**Analyzing Nutritional Deficiencies**\n\nI'm now extracting information related to nutritional deficiencies. I have successfully gathered the definitions, and key symptoms, and classifications, focusing on Kwashiorkor, Marasmus, and Marasmic Kwashiorkor, and key examples such as Scurvy. I've also detailed the two examples, Scurvy and Rickets, highlighting key clinical features. I am prioritizing a structured layout for easy access.\n\n\n**Extracting Maternal Care Data**\n\nI'm now focused on antenatal and postnatal care. I've successfully extracted definitions, core objectives, and common signs for antenatal care and postnatal care. I've also pulled the definition of menopause and its common symptoms, as well as the definition and major causes, both female and male factors, of infertility. I'm focusing on organizing this information for later comparison and review.\n\n\n**Defining Urinary Incontinence**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on urinary incontinence and related topics. I've extracted the definition, \"the involuntary uncontrollable loss or leakage of urine that is objectively demonstrable and causes physical hygiene emotional or social impairment,\" and also major types, including stress, urge, overflow, and functional incontinence. I've also pulled the definition of a hysterectomy.\n\n\n**Defining Gynecological Procedures**\n\nI've extracted definitions and indications for dilatation and curettage (D&C), focusing on diagnostic and therapeutic applications. I have also extracted the definition and the initial signs for pre-eclampsia. I'm prioritizing clear organization of each indication.\n\n\n"}
{"step_index":9,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-06T13:20:36Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nMedical & Allied Health Sciences: Master Examination Guide\r\nComprehensive 32-Question Master Bank (05-Mark Long-Form Model Answers)\r\nQUESTIONS 01 – 32 5-MARK EXAMINATION FORMAT SYSTEMIC PATHOLOGY • CRITICAL CARE • DERMATOLOGY • OB-GYN\r\nPART I: RESPIRATORY PATHOLOGY & PULMONARY MEDICINE (Q01 – Q07)\r\nQ 01 Acute Bronchitis\r\nDefinition: An acute, self-limiting inflammation of the tracheobronchial mucosa presenting characteristically with persistent cough with or\r\nwithout sputum production.\r\nEtiology:\r\nViral (>90%): Influenza A/B, Parainfluenza, RSV, Rhinovirus, Adenovirus, Coronavirus.\r\nBacterial (<10%):Mycoplasma pneumoniae, Chlamydophila pneumoniae, Bordetella pertussis.\r\nNon-Infectious: Chemical fumes, dusts, tobacco smoke, air pollutants.\r\nClinical Features: Persistent cough (initially dry/hacking, later mucoid or purulent, lasting 1–3 weeks); retrosternal burning soreness on\r\ncoughing; low-grade fever, malaise, myalgias; scattered expiratory wheezes/rhonchi and coarse crackles that clear upon coughing.\r\nInvestigations: Primarily clinical diagnosis. Chest X-ray (PA view) is usually normal; indicated to exclude pneumonia if tachypnea,\r\ntachycardia, high fever, or consolidation signs are present. Sputum PCR only for suspected B. pertussis.\r\nComplications: Secondary bronchopneumonia, acute asthma/COPD exacerbation, chronic post-infectious bronchial\r\nhyperresponsiveness (>4 weeks).\r\nManagement: Supportive hydration and steam inhalation; Paracetamol/Ibuprofen for chest soreness and fever; Dextromethorphan for\r\ndisruptive dry cough; inhaled Salbutamol for bronchospasm/wheeze. Routine antibiotics are not recommended unless elderly with\r\ncomorbidities or confirmed pertussis (Azithromycin).\r\nQ 02 Bronchial Pneumonia (Bronchopneumonia)\r\nDefinition: Acute suppurative inflammation of the pulmonary parenchyma centered around terminal and respiratory bronchioles,\r\npresenting with patchy, multifocal consolidation involving multiple pulmonary lobes.\r\nCauses: Bacterial: Streptococcus pneumoniae, Staphylococcus aureus, Klebsiella pneumoniae, Pseudomonas aeruginosa, H.\r\ninfluenzae. Predisposing factors include extremes of age, aspiration, malnutrition, post-viral airway injury, and immobilization.\r\nClinical Presentation: High-grade fever with chills/rigors, productive cough with purulent/rust-colored sputum, progressive dyspnea,\r\npleuritic chest pain; tachypnea, localized dullness on percussion, bronchial breath sounds, increased vocal resonance, and coarse\r\ninspiratory crackles.\r\nInvestigations: Chest X-ray (shows bilateral patchy, mottled opacities centered around bronchi); Sputum Gram stain and culture/\r\nsensitivity; blood cultures; CBC (leukocytosis with left shift, elevated CRP/ESR); ABG for hypoxemia.\r\nComplications: Parapneumonic effusion, empyema thoracis, lung abscess formation, sepsis/septic shock, Type 1 respiratory failure,\r\nand ARDS.\r\nTreatment: Supplemental O2 (target SpO2 ≥92%); IV fluids and chest physiotherapy; Empiric antibiotics: IV Ceftriaxone or Amoxicillin\r\nclavulanate plus Azithromycin (community-acquired); Piperacillin-tazobactam or Meropenem plus Vancomycin for hospital-acquired/\r\nMRSA; tailored to culture sensitivities for 7–10 days.\r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\nHIGH-YIELD MEDICAL REVISION (05-MARK QUESTIONS) QUESTIONS 01 – 32 MASTER COMPREHENSIVE BANK\r\nMedical & Allied Health Sciences Examination Series • 5-Mark Long-Form Outlines Page 1 of 12\r\nQ 03 Pleural Effusion\r\nDefinition: Pathological accumulation of excess fluid within the pleural cavity (>15 mL) resulting from an imbalance between fluid\r\nformation and absorption.\r\nTypes & Causes:\r\nTransudative (Altered Starling forces): Congestive heart failure, liver cirrhosis with ascites, nephrotic syndrome, severe\r\nhypoalbuminemia.\r\nExudative (Inflammatory/vascular permeability): Parapneumonic infection, tuberculosis, bronchogenic/metastatic carcinoma,\r\npulmonary infarction/PE, autoimmune diseases (SLE, RA).\r\nClinical Features: Progressive exertional dyspnea, pleuritic chest pain, dry cough; decreased chest expansion on affected side,\r\ncontralateral mediastinal shift (massive effusion), stony dull percussion note, absent/diminished breath sounds and vocal resonance.\r\nInvestigations: Chest X-ray (costophrenic angle blunting >175 mL, homogenous opacity with lateral upward-curving meniscus sign);\r\nThoracic Ultrasound; Diagnostic thoracocentesis using Light's Criteria for exudates (Fluid/Serum Protein >0.5, Fluid/Serum LDH >0.6,\r\nor Fluid LDH >2/3 upper limit of serum LDH); ADA (>40 U/L indicates TB), cytology, and microbiology.\r\nManagement: Treat etiology (diuretics for CHF; ATT for TB; chemotherapy for malignancy); Therapeutic thoracocentesis (drain ≤1,000\r\n1,500 mL/session); Intercostal chest drain (ICD) for empyema (pH <7.20) or hemothorax; Chemical pleurodesis (talc, bleomycin) for\r\nrecurrent malignant effusions.\r\nQ 04 Pneumothorax\r\nDefinition: Accumulation of free air within the pleural space between the parietal and visceral pleura, causing partial or complete lung\r\ncollapse.\r\nTypes & Causes:\r\nSpontaneous: Primary (rupture of subpleural apical blebs in tall, young men); Secondary (COPD bullae, cavitary TB, cystic fibrosis).\r\nTraumatic / Iatrogenic: Penetrating/blunt chest trauma, rib fractures, subclavian line placement, pleural biopsy, mechanical\r\nbarotrauma.\r\nTension Pneumothorax: One-way valve mechanism allowing air entry during inspiration but trapping air on expiration, generating\r\nmassive intrapleural pressure.\r\nClinical Features: Sudden unilateral sharp pleuritic chest pain and dyspnea; decreased expansion, hyperresonant percussion note,\r\nabsent/diminished breath sounds. Tension hallmark: cyanosis, hypotension, tachycardia, tracheal deviation to the contralateral side, and\r\ndistended neck veins.\r\nComplications: Obstructive shock, severe hypoxemic respiratory failure, hemopneumothorax, re-expansion pulmonary edema.\r\nEmergency Management:\r\nTension Pneumothorax: Immediate emergency needle decompression (14–16G cannula in 2nd ICS midclavicular line or 4th/5th ICS\r\nanterior axillary line) without awaiting X-ray.\r\nDefinitive Tube Thoracostomy: Intercostal chest tube (ICD) with underwater seal inserted in the safe triangle.\r\nHigh-flow supplemental oxygen (accelerates pleural air resorption); VATS pleurodesis for persistent leaks (>48h) or recurrence.\r\nQ 05 Lung Abscess\r\nDefinition: Localized parenchymal microbial necrosis and cavity formation containing purulent necrotic debris and fluid.\r\nEtiology: Aspiration of oral/gastric secretions containing anaerobes (Peptostreptococcus, Bacteroides, Fusobacterium) in patients with\r\nimpaired consciousness (alcoholism, stroke, epilepsy) or poor dentition; Necrotizing pneumonia (S. aureus, Klebsiella); Bronchial\r\nobstruction by carcinoma; Septic emboli (tricuspid endocarditis).\r\nClinical Manifestations: High swinging fever with rigors and night sweats, malaise, progressive weight loss; cough productive of\r\ncopious, foul-smelling, putrid purulent sputum; hemoptysis; pallor, rapid finger clubbing, dullness to percussion, and cavernous/amphoric\r\nbreath sounds over cavity.\r\nInvestigations: Chest X-ray (thick-walled solitary cavity with a prominent air-fluid level surrounded by consolidation); CECT Chest\r\n(delineates cavity and excludes empyema); Sputum microbiology (Gram, AFB, culture); Bronchoscopy to exclude obstructing\r\nendobronchial neoplasm.\r\nComplications: Empyema/pyopneumothorax, bronchopleural fistula, massive fatal hemoptysis, metastatic brain abscess, secondary\r\namyloidosis.\r\nTreatment: Prolonged antimicrobial therapy (4–8 weeks): IV Ampicillin-sulbactam, Amoxicillin-clavulanate, or Clindamycin, transitioned\r\nto oral; Postural drainage and chest physiotherapy; Percutaneous catheter drainage for refractory cavities >6 cm; Surgical lobectomy for\r\nmassive hemoptysis or suspected underlying malignancy.\r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\nHIGH-YIELD MEDICAL REVISION (05-MARK QUESTIONS) QUESTIONS 01 – 32 MASTER COMPREHENSIVE BANK\r\nMedical & Allied Health Sciences Examination Series • 5-Mark Long-Form Outlines Page 2 of 12\r\nQ 06 Respiratory Failure\r\nDefinition: Inability of the respiratory system to maintain adequate gas exchange, defined as arterial PaO2 <60 mmHg with or without\r\nPaCO2 >50 mmHg on ambient air at sea level.\r\nTypes & Causes:\r\nType 1 (Hypoxemic): PaO2 <60 mmHg with normal/low PaCO2. Caused by V/Q mismatch and right-to-left shunting: ARDS, severe\r\npneumonia, acute pulmonary edema, massive PE, diffuse interstitial fibrosis.\r\nType 2 (Hypercapnic): PaCO2 >50 mmHg with companion hypoxemia. Caused by alveolar hypoventilation/pump failure: severe\r\nCOPD, acute severe asthma, opioid overdose, myasthenia gravis, Guillain-Barré, severe kyphoscoliosis.\r\nClinical Features: Hypoxemia: dyspnea, tachypnea, tachycardia, central cyanosis, restlessness, confusion. Hypercapnia: bounding\r\npulses, warm flushed hands, morning headache, asterixis (flapping tremor), somnolence, and CO2 narcosis/coma.\r\nInvestigations: Arterial Blood Gas (ABG) analysis (confirms diagnosis, defines acidosis/alkalosis and compensation); Continuous pulse\r\noximetry; Chest X-ray and CT thorax; Spirometry.\r\nManagement: Controlled oxygen therapy (target SpO2 94–98% in Type 1; strictly 88–92% using a Venturi mask in Type 2 COPD to\r\npreserve hypoxic drive); Non-Invasive Ventilation (BiPAP) for hypercapnic COPD exacerbations; Invasive mechanical ventilation if severe\r\nacidosis (pH <7.25), exhaustion, or coma; Bronchodilators, systemic steroids, diuretics, and antidotes (Naloxone).\r\nQ 07 Occupational Lung Diseases\r\nDefinition: Preventable non-neoplastic and neoplastic disorders caused by repeated inhalation of mineral dusts, organic particles,\r\nfumes, or toxic gases in the work environment.\r\nCauses & Common Types:\r\nSilicosis: Inhalation of crystalline SiO2 in stone cutting, sandblasting, quarrying, foundry work; produces fibrotic silicotic nodules and\r\nhigh risk of tuberculosis (silicotuberculosis).\r\nAsbestosis: Inhalation of asbestos fibers in shipbuilding, demolition, roofing; causes bibasilar interstitial fibrosis, calcified pleural\r\nplaques, and malignant mesothelioma.\r\nCoal Workers' Pneumoconiosis (CWP): Coal dust inhalation leading to anthracosis and progressive massive fibrosis.\r\nOrganic: Byssinosis (cotton dust, \"Monday chest tightness\"), Farmer's lung (thermophilic actinomycetes in moldy hay).\r\nClinical Features: Insidious exertional dyspnea, chronic dry or productive cough (melanoptysis in CWP), wheezing, bibasilar end\r\ninspiratory crackles, reduced chest expansion, digital clubbing, and late cor pulmonale.\r\nPrevention: Engineering controls (wet drilling, local exhaust ventilation, toxic material substitution); Administrative controls (worker\r\nrotation); PPE (certified N95/HEPA particulate respirators); Periodic medical screening with spirometry and chest X-rays (ILO\r\nclassification).\r\nManagement: Immediate permanent removal from dust exposure; Bronchodilators and supplemental O2; Pneumococcal and influenza\r\nvaccination; Tuberculosis screening; Pulmonary rehabilitation; Medico-legal compensation.\r\nPART II: CARDIOVASCULAR PATHOLOGY & HEMODYNAMICS (Q08 – Q15)\r\nQ 08 Atherosclerosis\r\nDefinition: Chronic inflammatory disease of large/medium muscular and elastic arteries characterized by intimal fibrofatty plaques\r\n(atheromas) narrowing the lumen and weakening the arterial wall.\r\nMajor Risk Factors: Modifiable: Dyslipidemia (high LDL, low HDL), systemic hypertension, cigarette smoking, diabetes mellitus, visceral\r\nobesity, sedentary life. Non-modifiable: Advancing age, male sex, family history of premature CAD.\r\nClinical Manifestations: Coronary: stable angina, acute myocardial infarction; Cerebral: TIAs, ischemic stroke; Peripheral: intermittent\r\nclaudication, critical limb ischemia; Renal: renovascular hypertension.\r\nComplications: Acute plaque rupture with occlusive luminal thrombosis, aneurysmal dilatation/rupture (abdominal aorta),\r\natheroembolism, and progressive ischemic cardiomyopathy.\r\nInvestigations: Fasting lipid profile, HbA1c, hs-CRP; ECG and treadmill exercise test (TMT); Carotid Doppler (intima-media thickness)\r\nand Ankle-Brachial Index (ABI <0.9); Coronary CT angiography and conventional catheter angiography.\r\nManagement: Lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, aerobic exercise, smoking cessation; Pharmacotherapy: High-intensity statins (Atorvastatin\r\n40–80 mg to achieve LDL <55 mg/dL), low-dose Aspirin (75–100 mg), ACE inhibitors, tight glycemic/BP control; Revascularization: PCI\r\nwith drug-eluting stents, CABG, or endarterectomy.\r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\nHIGH-YIELD MEDICAL REVISION (05-MARK QUESTIONS) QUESTIONS 01 – 32 MASTER COMPREHENSIVE BANK\r\nMedical & Allied Health Sciences Examination Series • 5-Mark Long-Form Outlines Page 3 of 12\r\nQ 09 Thrombosis and Embolism\r\nDefinitions: Thrombosis is the intravascular formation of a solid/semisolid mass of fibrin, platelets, and cells in intact unruptured vessels\r\nduring life. Embolism is the mechanical occlusion of a vessel by a detached intravascular mass transported away from its origin.\r\nCauses (Virchow's Triad): 1. Endothelial injury (atherosclerosis, trauma, vasculitis); 2. Altered blood flow (venous stasis from\r\nimmobility, turbulence from aneurysms); 3. Hypercoagulability (Factor V Leiden, malignancy, antiphospholipid syndrome, oral\r\ncontraceptives). Emboli arise from dislodged DVTs, cardiac mural thrombi (atrial fibrillation), fat (fractures), air, or septic vegetations.\r\nClinical Features: DVT: Unilateral calf swelling, calf pain, erythema, tenderness, positive Homans sign; Pulmonary Embolism: acute\r\ndyspnea, pleuritic chest pain, tachypnea, tachycardia, hemoptysis, syncope; Acute Arterial Embolism (6 Ps): Pain, Pallor, Pulselessness,\r\nParesthesia, Paralysis, Poikilothermia.\r\nComplications: Fatal massive PE with obstructive shock, ischemic stroke, acute mesenteric ischemia, post-thrombotic syndrome.\r\nDiagnosis: Compression Duplex ultrasound (DVT); quantitative D-dimer assay; CT Pulmonary Angiography (CTPA, gold standard for\r\nPE); Arterial Doppler and CT angiography.\r\nManagement: Anticoagulation: LMWH (Enoxaparin 1 mg/kg SC q12h) or unfractionated heparin, transitioned to DOACs (Apixaban,\r\nRivaroxaban) or Warfarin (target INR 2.0–3.0) for 3–6 months; Thrombolysis (r-tPA / Alteplase) for massive PE with shock or acute\r\nischemic stroke <4.5h; Catheter thrombectomy, surgical embolectomy, and IVC filter placement.\r\nQ 10 Ischemic Heart Disease (IHD)\r\nDefinition: Cardiac syndromes resulting from an imbalance between myocardial oxygen supply and demand, predominantly caused by\r\nfixed atherosclerotic narrowing of epicardial coronary arteries.\r\nRisk Factors: Dyslipidemia, hypertension, cigarette smoking, diabetes mellitus, visceral obesity, metabolic syndrome, male sex, older\r\nage, family history.\r\nClinical Features: Stable angina: retrosternal tightness/pressure radiating to left arm/neck/jaw, provoked by exertion, relieved by rest or\r\nsublingual nitroglycerin within 2–5 min; Acute Coronary Syndromes (NSTEMI/STEMI): severe crushing retrosternal pain lasting >20 min,\r\noccurring at rest, accompanied by diaphoresis, nausea, dyspnea, and syncope.\r\nInvestigations: 12-lead ECG (ST elevation in STEMI, ST depression/T inversion in ischemia/NSTEMI); Cardiac biomarkers (hs-cTnI, hs\r\ncTnT, CK-MB); Echocardiography (regional wall motion abnormalities, LVEF); Coronary angiography (gold standard).\r\nComplications: Malignant ventricular arrhythmias (VT/VF), sudden cardiac death, cardiogenic shock, congestive heart failure, papillary\r\nmuscle rupture with acute mitral regurgitation, ventricular free-wall/septal rupture.\r\nTreatment: Acute ACS: Oxygen (if SpO2 <90%), sublingual Nitroglycerin, chewable Aspirin (300 mg) plus P2Y12 inhibitor (Ticagrelor\r\n180 mg or Clopidogrel 600 mg), Enoxaparin; Primary PCI (<90 min door-to-balloon) or thrombolysis (<120 min); Long-term: dual\r\nantiplatelet therapy, high-intensity statin, cardioselective beta-blocker, and ACE inhibitor.\r\nQ 11 Cardiac Failure (Heart Failure)\r\nDefinition: Complex clinical syndrome resulting from structural or functional impairment of ventricular filling or blood ejection, leaving the\r\nheart unable to meet the metabolic demands of tissues at normal venous pressures.\r\nTypes & Causes:\r\nTypes: HFrEF (systolic, LVEF ≤40%); HFpEF (diastolic, LVEF ≥50%); Left-sided vs. Right-sided; Acute vs. Chronic.\r\nCauses: Coronary artery disease and myocardial infarction (most common), chronic hypertension, valvular heart disease, dilated\r\ncardiomyopathies, cor pulmonale.\r\nClinical Manifestations: Left-sided (Pulmonary congestion): exertional dyspnea, orthopnea, paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea (PND),\r\nbibasilar crackles, S3 gallop; Right-sided (Systemic venous congestion): elevated JVP, bilateral pitting pedal edema, congestive\r\nhepatomegaly, ascites.\r\nComplications: Acute pulmonary edema, ventricular arrhythmias, cardiorenal syndrome, cardiac cirrhosis, mural thrombi with systemic\r\nembolism.\r\nManagement: Lifestyle: dietary sodium (<2 g/day) and fluid restriction, weight monitoring; Guideline-Directed Medical Therapy (GDMT \r\n4 Pillars for HFrEF): 1. ARNI (Sacubitril-valsartan) or ACEi/ARB; 2. Beta-blocker (Carvedilol, Bisoprolol, Metoprolol succinate); 3. MRA\r\n(Spironolactone); 4. SGLT2 inhibitor (Dapagliflozin/Empagliflozin); Diuretics (Furosemide) for volume overload; Device therapy: ICD and\r\nCRT-D; LVAD or heart transplantation.\r\n• \r\n• \r\nHIGH-YIELD MEDICAL REVISION (05-MARK QUESTIONS) QUESTIONS 01 – 32 MASTER COMPREHENSIVE BANK\r\nMedical & Allied Health Sciences Examination Series • 5-Mark Long-Form Outlines Page 4 of 12\r\nQ 12 Rheumatic Heart Disease (RHD)\r\nDefinition: Chronic permanent cardiac valvular damage resulting from single or recurrent episodes of acute rheumatic fever (ARF)\r\ninitiated by untreated Group A β-hemolytic streptococcal (GAS) pharyngitis.\r\nEtiology & Pathogenesis:Streptococcus pyogenes. Driven by molecular mimicry: streptococcal M-protein cross-reacts with human\r\ncardiac myosin and sarcolemma, producing pancarditis with pathognomonic granulomatous Aschoff bodies.\r\nClinical Features: History of ARF (Jones criteria). Valvular involvement (Mitral >90%, then Aortic): \r\nMitral Stenosis: Exertional dyspnea, hemoptysis, malar flush, tapping apex beat, loud S1, opening snap, low-pitched rumbling mid\r\ndiastolic murmur at the apex.\r\nMitral Regurgitation: Holosystolic murmur radiating to axilla; Aortic lesions cause early diastolic decrescendo murmur.\r\nComplications: Congestive heart failure, atrial fibrillation, left atrial mural thrombi with stroke, and infective endocarditis.\r\nInvestigations: Doppler Echocardiography (shows thickened valve leaflets, chordal shortening, \"hockey-stick\" anterior mitral leaflet,\r\npressure gradients); 12-lead ECG (left atrial enlargement / P-mitrale, AF); Chest X-ray (straightening of left heart border, double right\r\ncontour); Elevated ASO titer.\r\nManagement: Secondary penicillin prophylaxis: Benzathine penicillin G (1.2 million units IM q3–4 weeks) for at least 10 years or until\r\nage 40; Medical: diuretics, beta-blockers/digoxin for AF, Warfarin anticoagulation (target INR 2.0–3.0); Surgery/Intervention:\r\nPercutaneous Transvenous Mitral Commissurotomy (PTMC) or surgical prosthetic valve replacement.\r\nQ 13 Cardiomyopathies\r\nDefinition: Heterogeneous group of intrinsic myocardial disorders associated with mechanical/electrical cardiac dysfunction, not\r\nsecondary to coronary disease, hypertension, or valvular disease.\r\nTypes & Causes:\r\nDilated (DCM): Ventricular dilatation with systolic dysfunction (LVEF <40%). Causes: idiopathic, titin mutations, viral myocarditis,\r\nchronic alcoholism, doxorubicin toxicity, peripartum.\r\nHypertrophic (HCM): Asymmetric septal hypertrophy with preserved/hyperdynamic systolic function. Autosomal dominant mutation\r\nin sarcomeric genes (β-MHC, MYBPC3).\r\nRestrictive (RCM): Rigid, non-compliant ventricular walls causing impaired diastolic filling. Infiltrative causes: amyloidosis,\r\nsarcoidosis, hemochromatosis.\r\nARVC: Fibrofatty replacement of RV myocardium predisposing to ventricular arrhythmias.\r\nClinical Manifestations: DCM: biventricular heart failure (dyspnea, edema, cardiomegaly, S3); HCM: dyspnea, chest pain, exertional\r\nsyncope, harsh systolic ejection murmur that intensifies with Valsalva; sudden cardiac death in athletes; RCM: severe right-sided failure,\r\nhigh JVP with Kussmaul's sign.\r\nInvestigations: Echocardiography (ventricular dilation in DCM; asymmetric septal thickness ≥15 mm with systolic anterior motion of\r\nmitral valve in HCM; bi-atrial dilatation in RCM); Cardiac MRI (Late Gadolinium Enhancement for fibrosis/amyloid); Holter ECG.\r\nTreatment: DCM: standard GDMT (ARNI, beta-blockers, MRA, SGLT2i), ICD/CRT; HCM: non-vasodilating beta-blockers (Metoprolol),\r\nVerapamil, Mavacamten, surgical septal myectomy, prophylactic ICD; RCM: cautious diuretics, Tafamidis for amyloidosis.\r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\nHIGH-YIELD MEDICAL REVISION (05-MARK QUESTIONS) QUESTIONS 01 – 32 MASTER COMPREHENSIVE BANK\r\nMedical & Allied Health Sciences Examination Series • 5-Mark Long-Form Outlines Page 5 of 12\r\nQ 14 Cardiac Arrest\r\nDefinition: Abrupt, unexpected cessation of mechanical cardiac pump activity resulting in circulatory collapse, sudden\r\nunresponsiveness, absent central arterial pulse, and apnea or agonal gasping.\r\nImmediate Assessment:\r\n1. Confirm scene safety; 2. Check responsiveness (tap shoulders and shout); 3. Simultaneously assess breathing and carotid pulse\r\n(minimum 5 sec, maximum 10 sec); 4. If pulseless and breathless, activate emergency response (Call Code Blue / EMS) and call for\r\nan AED.\r\nEmergency Management:\r\nHigh-Quality CPR (C-A-B): Rate 100–120/min, depth 5–6 cm, allow full recoil, minimize interruptions, 30:2 compression-ventilation\r\nratio.\r\nDefibrillation for Shockable Rhythms (VF / Pulseless VT): Deliver 1 unsynchronized shock (120–200 J biphasic or 360 J\r\nmonophasic); resume CPR immediately for 2 minutes without pulse check; Epinephrine 1 mg IV q3–5min after 2nd shock;\r\nAmiodarone 300 mg IV (second dose 150 mg) for shock-refractory VF/pVT.\r\nNon-Shockable Rhythms (Asystole / PEA): CPR in 2-min cycles; Epinephrine 1 mg IV immediately and q3–5min; Defibrillation is \r\ncontraindicated.\r\nTreat Reversible Causes (Hs & Ts): Hypovolemia, Hypoxia, Hydrogen ion (acidosis), Hypo/hyperkalemia, Hypothermia; Tension\r\npneumothorax, Tamponade, Toxins, Thrombosis (coronary/pulmonary).\r\nPost-ROSC Care: Hemodynamic support (MAP ≥65 mmHg), targeted temperature management (32–36°C for 24h), emergency\r\ncoronary angiography.\r\nQ 15 Cardiopulmonary Resuscitation (CPR) & Basic Life Support (BLS)\r\nPrinciples: Maintains artificial coronary and cerebral perfusion via rhythmic external chest compressions and ventilations to preserve\r\ntissue viability until ROSC is achieved. Follows the C-A-B sequence (Chest compressions prioritized over Airway and Breathing). Chain\r\nof Survival: Early Recognition & Activation → High-Quality CPR → Rapid Defibrillation → Advanced Resuscitation → Post-Arrest Care →\r\nRecovery.\r\nCore Metrics of High-Quality CPR: Rate: 100–120/min; Depth: 5–6 cm (adult); Full chest recoil between compressions (avoid leaning);\r\nChest compression fraction >60% (target >80%); Avoid hyperventilation (500–600 mL tidal volume visible chest rise over 1 second).\r\nStep-by-Step BLS Procedure:\r\n1. Confirm scene safety; 2. Check responsiveness; 3. Call for help, activate EMS / Code Blue, retrieve AED; 4. Check carotid pulse\r\nand breathing (5–10 sec); 5. If no pulse: begin chest compressions on lower half of sternum, interlocked fingers, elbows locked,\r\nshoulders vertical; 6. Open airway (Head-Tilt–Chin-Lift; Jaw-Thrust if spinal trauma); 7. Deliver 2 rescue breaths after every 30\r\ncompressions (30:2 ratio); 8. Attach AED as soon as available, clear patient during rhythm analysis, deliver shock if indicated, and\r\nimmediately resume compressions; 9. Switch rescuers every 2 minutes (5 cycles) to prevent fatigue.\r\nPART III: CRITICAL CARE, DIAGNOSTICS & EMERGENCY SURGERY (Q16 – Q21)\r\nQ 16 Mechanical Ventilation\r\nDefinition: Supportive intervention using an automated positive-pressure device to assist or replace spontaneous alveolar ventilation\r\nand pulmonary gas exchange.\r\nIndications: Severe hypoxemic respiratory failure (ARDS, pulmonary edema); Hypercapnic respiratory failure (severe COPD,\r\nmyasthenic crisis); Airway protection / coma (GCS ≤8); General anesthesia and shock states to reduce work of breathing.\r\nBasic Principles: Positive pressure ventilation pushes gas into lungs. Parameters: Tidal Volume (Vt, 6–8 mL/kg; 4–6 mL/kg in ARDS\r\nlung-protective strategy), Respiratory Rate (12–16/min), FiO2 (21–100%), PEEP (5–15 cmH2O to prevent alveolar collapse), I:E ratio\r\n(1:2).\r\nTypes & Modes: Non-Invasive (NIV: CPAP, BiPAP via mask); Invasive (via ETT/tracheostomy): Volume-Controlled (VCV), Pressure\r\nControlled (PCV), SIMV, and Pressure Support Ventilation (PSV).\r\nComplications: Ventilator-Associated Pneumonia (VAP >48h post-intubation), Barotrauma/Volutrauma (pneumothorax,\r\npneumomediastinum), Hemodynamic hypotension (reduced venous return from positive intrathoracic pressure), and Tracheal stenosis.\r\nMonitoring: Continuous pulse oximetry, capnography (EtCO2), serial ABGs; Peak airway pressure (<35 cmH2O), Plateau pressure\r\n(Pplat ≤30 cmH2O); Sedation scoring (RASS), ETT depth.\r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\nHIGH-YIELD MEDICAL REVISION (05-MARK QUESTIONS) QUESTIONS 01 – 32 MASTER COMPREHENSIVE BANK\r\nMedical & Allied Health Sciences Examination Series • 5-Mark Long-Form Outlines Page 6 of 12\r\nQ 17 Arterial Blood Gas (ABG) Analysis\r\nDefinition & Significance: Quantitative point-of-care laboratory assessment performed on heparinized arterial blood (radial artery)\r\nmeasuring gas partial pressures and pH to assess oxygenation, alveolar ventilation, and acid-base status.\r\nNormal Ranges: pH: 7.35–7.45; PaCO2: 35–45 mmHg; HCO3-: 22–26 mEq/L; PaO2: 80–100 mmHg; Base Excess: -2 to +2 mEq/L;\r\nSaO2: 95–100%.\r\nInterpretation of Primary Disorders:\r\nRespiratory Acidosis: pH <7.35, PaCO2 >45 mmHg. Due to hypoventilation (COPD, asthma, opioid overdose, neuromuscular\r\nweakness).\r\nRespiratory Alkalosis: pH >7.45, PaCO2 <35 mmHg. Due to hyperventilation (anxiety/panic, pulmonary embolism, early salicylate\r\ntoxicity, sepsis).\r\nMetabolic Acidosis: pH <7.35, HCO3- <22 mEq/L. High Anion Gap (DKA, lactic acidosis, uremia, toxic alcohols); Normal Anion Gap\r\n(severe diarrhea, RTA).\r\nMetabolic Alkalosis: pH >7.45, HCO3- >26 mEq/L. Due to H+ loss (severe vomiting, NG suction, loop diuretics,\r\nhyperaldosteronism).\r\nQ 18 Electrocardiogram (ECG)\r\nBasic Principles: Non-invasive graphic recording of cardiac bioelectrical potentials and vector voltages produced by myocardial\r\ndepolarization and repolarization, measured via 10 skin electrodes arranged in 12 standard leads (6 limb, 6 precordial).\r\nMajor Waves, Complexes & Intervals:\r\nP wave: Atrial depolarization (<0.12s, <2.5 mm).\r\nPR interval: AV nodal conduction delay (0.12–0.20s). Prolonged in 1st-degree AV block; short in WPW.\r\nQRS complex: Ventricular depolarization (<0.12s). Widened in bundle branch blocks and VT.\r\nST segment: Isoelectric plateau phase. Elevation indicates STEMI/pericarditis; depression indicates ischemia/NSTEMI.\r\nT wave: Ventricular repolarization. Peaked in hyperkalemia; inverted/flat in ischemia or hypokalemia.\r\nQT interval: Total ventricular electrical cycle (depolarization + repolarization; QTc ≤0.44s men, ≤0.46s women).\r\nClinical Significance: Diagnostic standard for Acute Coronary Syndromes (STEMI vs. NSTEMI); dysrhythmias (AF, VT, VF, heart\r\nblocks); chamber enlargement (LVH); electrolyte disorders (hyper/hypokalemia); and drug toxicities (digoxin).\r\nQ 19 Shock\r\nDefinition: Life-threatening acute circulatory failure characterized by inadequate tissue and cellular microvascular perfusion, resulting in\r\ncellular hypoxia, anaerobic metabolism (lactic acidosis), and multi-organ failure.\r\nTypes & Causes:\r\nHypovolemic: Hemorrhagic (trauma, GI bleed); Non-hemorrhagic (burn plasma loss, severe dehydration).\r\nCardiogenic: Myocardial infarction (>40% LV loss), end-stage cardiomyopathy, severe acute mitral regurgitation.\r\nDistributive: Septic shock (bacterial endotoxemia), anaphylactic shock (Type 1 hypersensitivity), neurogenic shock (high spinal cord\r\ninjury).\r\nObstructive: Tension pneumothorax, massive pulmonary embolism, cardiac tamponade.\r\nClinical Features: Systemic hypotension (SBP <90 mmHg or MAP <65 mmHg), tachycardia (>100 bpm; bradycardia in neurogenic),\r\ntachypnea, cold clammy pale skin with delayed capillary refill (>3s; warm flushed in early septic shock), progressive oliguria (<0.5 mL/kg/\r\nh), confusion/coma.\r\nComplications: Acute kidney injury, ARDS, DIC, mesenteric ischemia, MODS, and death.\r\nManagement: Airway & high-flow O2; IV access (two 14–16G cannulas) & fluid resuscitation (30 mL/kg balanced crystalloids for\r\nhypovolemia/sepsis; restrict in cardiogenic shock); Norepinephrine (first-line vasopressor targeting MAP ≥65 mmHg); Dobutamine for\r\ncardiogenic inotropic support; Epinephrine IM (0.3–0.5 mg 1:1,000) for anaphylaxis; Emergency cause-specific relief: blood transfusion\r\nfor trauma, needle decompression for tension pneumothorax, pericardiocentesis for tamponade, PCI for cardiogenic MI.\r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\nHIGH-YIELD MEDICAL REVISION (05-MARK QUESTIONS) QUESTIONS 01 – 32 MASTER COMPREHENSIVE BANK\r\nMedical & Allied Health Sciences Examination Series • 5-Mark Long-Form Outlines Page 7 of 12\r\nQ 20 Burns\r\nClassification:\r\nDepth: Superficial (1st degree: red, dry, painful, heals <6d); Superficial partial-thickness (2nd degree: pink, weeping, thin blisters,\r\nvery painful); Deep partial-thickness (mottled white/red, reduced sensation, scarring); Full-thickness (3rd degree: leathery, charred,\r\ndry, anesthetic); 4th degree (involves muscle/bone).\r\nWallace's Rule of Nines (% TBSA in adults): Head & neck: 9%; Each arm: 9%; Anterior trunk: 18%; Posterior trunk: 18%; Each leg:\r\n18%; Perineum: 1%. (Patient's palm = 1%).\r\nClinical Assessment: Primary survey (ABCDE); Rule out inhalation injury (facial burns, singed nasal hairs, soot in sputum, stridor);\r\nCalculate % TBSA (excluding 1st degree).\r\nComplications: Hypovolemic burn shock, carbon monoxide toxicity, compartment syndrome from circumferential eschar, burn wound\r\nsepsis (Pseudomonas, S. aureus), Curling's stress ulcer, contractures, Marjolin's carcinoma.\r\nPrinciples of Management:\r\nStop burning, cool with tap water (15–20 min, no ice); Early intubation if inhalation injury suspected.\r\nParkland Formula Resuscitation (>15–20% TBSA): Total Ringer's Lactate in 24 hours = 4 mL × Weight (kg) × % TBSA. Give 50%\r\nin first 8 hours (from burn time), remaining 50% over next 16 hours. Titrate to urine output (0.5–1.0 mL/kg/h).\r\nWound debridement, topical Silver Sulfadiazine cream; IV Morphine; Tetanus prophylaxis; Emergency escharotomy for circumferential\r\nchest/limb burns; Early tangential excision and skin grafting (3–7 days).\r\nQ 21 Septicemia\r\nDefinition: Life-threatening systemic infection characterized by the active multiplication and persistence of pathogenic microbes and\r\ntoxins in the bloodstream, triggering a dysregulated host response and acute end-organ dysfunction.\r\nCauses: Gram-negative bacilli (E. coli, Klebsiella, Pseudomonas, Acinetobacter); Gram-positive cocci (S. aureus/MRSA, S.\r\npneumoniae, Enterococcus); Fungi (Candida). Primary foci: pneumonia, urosepsis, intra-abdominal peritonitis, central line infections.\r\nClinical Manifestations: High swinging fever with rigors or hypothermia (<36°C), resting tachycardia (>90 bpm), tachypnea (>20 bpm),\r\nhypotension (SBP <90 mmHg), warm flushed skin transitioning to cold mottled cyanosis, petechiae/purpura, delirium, and oliguria.\r\nComplications: Septic shock (hypotension requiring vasopressors with serum lactate >2 mmol/L), DIC, ARDS, acute tubular necrosis,\r\nand MODS.\r\nInvestigations: Blood cultures (2 sets drawn prior to antibiotics), paired sputum/urine cultures; CBC (leukocytosis >12,000/μL or\r\nleukopenia <4,000/μL, thrombocytopenia); Serum lactate (>2 mmol/L); Elevated Procalcitonin and CRP; PT/INR, aPTT, D-dimer.\r\nManagement (Surviving Sepsis Hour-1 Bundle):\r\n1. Measure blood lactate; 2. Blood cultures before antibiotics; 3. Administer empiric broad-spectrum IV antimicrobials within 1 hour\r\n(e.g., Piperacillin-tazobactam plus Vancomycin); 4. Rapid 30 mL/kg balanced crystalloids for hypotension or lactate ≥4 mmol/L; 5.\r\nNorepinephrine to maintain MAP ≥65 mmHg; 6. Prompt infectious source control (drain abscesses, remove infected lines).\r\nPART IV: DERMATOLOGY & CLINICAL NUTRITION (Q22 – Q26)\r\nQ 22 Psoriasis\r\nEtiology & Pathogenesis: Chronic immune-mediated dermatosis characterized by accelerated epidermal cell turnover (3–5 days vs.\r\nnormal 28 days) driven by T-helper lymphocytes (Th1/Th17) releasing TNF-α, IL-17, and IL-23. Strongly linked to HLA-Cw6. Triggers:\r\nphysical trauma (Koebner phenomenon), streptococcal pharyngitis, stress, drugs (beta-blockers, lithium, NSAIDs, steroid withdrawal).\r\nClinical Features: Sharply demarcated salmon-pink erythematous plaques covered by silvery-white micaceous scales on extensor\r\nsurfaces (knees, elbows, scalp, sacrum). Signs: Auspitz sign (pinpoint bleeding on removing scales) and Koebner phenomenon. Nail\r\nchanges (pitting, onycholysis, subungual hyperkeratosis, oil-drop sign). Variants: plaque, guttate, inverse, pustular, and erythrodermic.\r\nComplications: Psoriatic arthritis (PsA in 30%), life-threatening erythroderma (>90% TBSA involvement), severe metabolic syndrome,\r\ncardiovascular disease, and depression.\r\nTreatment: Topical: potent corticosteroids (Clobetasol), Calcipotriol (vitamin D3 analogue), keratolytics (salicylic acid), calcineurin\r\ninhibitors (tacrolimus for flexures); Phototherapy: Narrowband UVB (311 nm) or PUVA; Systemic: Methotrexate (7.5–25 mg/week),\r\nCyclosporine, Acitretin; Biologics: anti-TNF (Adalimumab), anti-IL-17 (Secukinumab), anti-IL-23 (Guselkumab).\r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\nHIGH-YIELD MEDICAL REVISION (05-MARK QUESTIONS) QUESTIONS 01 – 32 MASTER COMPREHENSIVE BANK\r\nMedical & Allied Health Sciences Examination Series • 5-Mark Long-Form Outlines Page 8 of 12\r\nQ 23 Eczema and Dermatitis\r\nDefinition & Causes: Non-contagious inflammatory skin condition histologically marked by epidermal spongiosis (intercellular edema)\r\nand intense pruritus. Driven by genetic skin barrier defects (filaggrin [FLG] mutations), Th2-skewed inflammation, elevated IgE, contact\r\nallergens (nickel, fragrances, rubber), chemical irritants, and S. aureus colonization.\r\nCommon Types: Atopic dermatitis (associated with asthma and allergic rhinitis); Contact dermatitis (Allergic [Type IV hypersensitivity]\r\nvs. Irritant [direct chemical/detergent disruption]); Seborrheic dermatitis (greasy scaly plaques on scalp/face linked to Malassezia);\r\nDyshidrotic eczema / pompholyx (pruritic vesicles on finger sides/palms/soles); Nummular / discoid eczema (coin-shaped plaques).\r\nClinical Features: Acute: intense itching, erythema, edema, clustered papules, weeping serous vesicles, and crusting. Chronic: dry\r\nxerotic skin, lichenification (thickened leathery skin with exaggerated markings from scratching), excoriations, and painful fissures.\r\nManagement: Trigger avoidance; liberal use of ceramide emollients immediately after bathing; Topical corticosteroids (hydrocortisone\r\nfor face; mometasone/betamethasone for body flares); Topical calcineurin inhibitors (Tacrolimus); Oral antihistamines for pruritus;\r\nPhototherapy (NB-UVB); Systemic immunosuppressants (Methotrexate, Cyclosporine) or Biologics (Dupilumab anti-IL-4Rα); Oral\r\nantibiotics for secondary impetiginization.\r\nQ 24 Bacterial, Fungal, and Viral Skin Infections\r\nBacterial Skin Infections:\r\nImpetigo (S. aureus, S. pyogenes): Thin-walled vesicles rupturing into pathognomonic honey-colored golden crusts on face/\r\nperioral zone. Rx: topical Mupirocin (2%) or oral Cephalexin.\r\nCellulitis & Erysipelas: Spreading erythema, warmth, pain, and edema with fever. Erysipelas is superficial with raised sharp borders;\r\ncellulitis is deep. Rx: Flucloxacillin, Cefazolin, or Vancomycin (MRSA).\r\nFolliculitis, Furuncles & Carbuncles (S. aureus): Suppurative hair follicle inflammation and necrotic boils. Rx: warm compresses,\r\nincision & drainage, anti-staphylococcal antibiotics.\r\nFungal Skin Infections:\r\nDermatophytosis (Tinea / Ringworm): Annular erythematous plaques with raised, scaly, advancing borders and central clearing\r\n(Tinea corporis, cruris, pedis, capitis). Rx: topical Terbinafine or Clotrimazole; oral Terbinafine/Itraconazole for extensive disease or\r\ntinea capitis (Griseofulvin).\r\nCandidiasis (C. albicans): Beefy-red intertriginous plaques with peripheral satellite pustules in moist body folds. Rx: keep dry,\r\ntopical Nystatin/Clotrimazole; oral Fluconazole.\r\nViral Skin Infections:\r\nHerpes Simplex (HSV-1/2): Grouped vesicles on an erythematous base (\"dewdrops on a rose petal\") that ulcerate painfully. Rx: oral\r\nAcyclovir (400 mg 5x/day) or Valacyclovir.\r\nHerpes Zoster (VZV reactivation): Unilateral, dermatomal eruption of lancinating pain and grouped vesicles. Rx: oral Valacyclovir\r\n(1,000 mg TID for 7d); Pregabalin for neuralgia.\r\nViral Warts (HPV 1, 2, 4): Hyperkeratotic exophytic papules with black dots (thrombosed capillaries). Rx: Salicylic acid, cryotherapy.\r\nQ 25 Vitamin and Mineral Deficiency Disorders\r\nMajor Vitamin Deficiencies & Manifestations:\r\nVitamin A: Nyctalopia (night blindness), Bitot's spots, xerophthalmia, keratomalacia (corneal ulceration and permanent blindness).\r\nVitamin B1 (Thiamine): Wet Beriberi (high-output heart failure, edema); Dry Beriberi (peripheral neuropathy); Wernicke-Korsakoff\r\nsyndrome (ataxia, ophthalmoplegia, memory loss).\r\nVitamin B3 (Niacin):Pellagra (4 Ds): Photosensitive Dermatitis (Casal's necklace), Diarrhea, Dementia, Death.\r\nVitamin C (Ascorbic acid):Scurvy: defective collagen cross-linking causes bleeding spongy gums, perifollicular petechiae,\r\ncorkscrew hairs, loose teeth, subperiosteal hematomas, delayed wound healing.\r\nVitamin D:Rickets in children (epiphyseal widening, bowed legs, rachitic rosary, delayed fontanelle closure); Osteomalacia in adults\r\n(diffuse bone pain, proximal muscle weakness, pseudofractures).\r\nMajor Mineral Deficiencies & Manifestations:\r\nIron: Microcytic hypochromic anemia, pallor, fatigue, angular cheilitis, atrophic glossitis, koilonychia (spoon nails), pica.\r\nIodine: Endemic goiter, hypothyroidism, Cretinism in infants (severe intellectual disability, dwarfism, deaf-mutism).\r\nZinc: Acrodermatitis enteropathica, impaired wound healing, blunted taste (hypogeusia), alopecia, immune dysfunction.\r\nManagement: Direct therapeutic micronutrient replacement (oral Ferrous sulfate with Vitamin C; Cholecalciferol 60,000 IU/week; IV/oral\r\nThiamine before glucose; Vitamin A capsules 200,000 IU; Ascorbic acid 500–1,000 mg); Food fortification (iodized salt, fortified flours);\r\nDietary diversification.\r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\nHIGH-YIELD MEDICAL REVISION (05-MARK QUESTIONS) QUESTIONS 01 – 32 MASTER COMPREHENSIVE BANK\r\nMedical & Allied Health Sciences Examination Series • 5-Mark Long-Form Outlines Page 9 of 12\r\nQ 26 Protein-Energy Malnutrition (PEM)\r\nDefinition & Causes: Spectrum of nutritional deficiency disorders resulting from inadequate intake/absorption of protein and caloric\r\nenergy to meet bodily growth and metabolic needs. Caused by poverty, food insecurity, faulty early weaning onto dilute starches,\r\nrecurrent gastroenteritis, and measles/TB infections.\r\nTypes & Clinical Features:\r\nKwashiorkor (Protein deficiency with adequate calories): Generalized bilateral pitting edema; \"moon face\"; \"crazy-pavement\" /\r\n\"flaky-paint\" dermatosis; sparse, dry, hypopigmented hair with alternating light/dark bands (flag sign); marked hepatomegaly (fatty\r\nliver infiltration); profound misery, apathy, and anorexia.\r\nMarasmus (Severe deficiency of both calories and protein): Severe somatic muscle wasting and total loss of subcutaneous fat;\r\nwizened, shrunken \"old-man\" facies; prominent ribs (\"accordion cage\"); loose folds of redundant skin over buttocks (\"baggy pants\"); \r\nabsence of edema; voracious appetite.\r\nMarasmic Kwashiorkor: Severe tissue wasting coexisting with peripheral pitting edema.\r\nComplications: Hypoglycemia (<54 mg/dL), hypothermia (<35.5°C), severe electrolyte derangements (hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia,\r\nsodium retention), occult severe sepsis, heart failure, and acute keratomalacia.\r\nManagement (WHO 10-Step Guidelines):\r\nStabilization (Days 1–7): Treat hypoglycemia (10% dextrose), warm child, gentle oral rehydration with ReSoMal (no rapid IV fluids);\r\nsupplement potassium/magnesium; broad-spectrum antibiotics; Vitamin A; initial starter feeding with low-protein F-75 diet (75 kcal/\r\n100 mL); strictly withhold iron during stabilization.\r\nRehabilitation (Weeks 2–6): Transition to energy-dense F-100 diet (100 kcal/100 mL) or RUTF for catch-up growth; add iron\r\nsupplementation; emotional stimulation.\r\nPART V: MATERNAL HEALTH, LABOR & GYNECOLOGY (Q27 – Q32)\r\nQ 27 Physiological Changes in Pregnancy, Antenatal & Postnatal Care\r\nPhysiological Changes During Pregnancy:\r\nCardiovascular: Cardiac output rises 30–50%; heart rate increases 10–15 bpm; SVR drops (progesterone-mediated vasodilation),\r\nlowering diastolic BP in 2nd trimester; supine hypotension syndrome from IVC compression.\r\nHematological: Plasma volume increases 50%, RBC mass rises 20–30% → physiological hemodilution anemia; hypercoagulable\r\nstate (elevated fibrinogen, factors VII, VIII, X → 5x increased VTE risk).\r\nRespiratory & Renal: Progesterone stimulates hyperventilation (mild respiratory alkalosis); GFR increases 50%, lowering serum\r\ncreatinine (<0.6 mg/dL).\r\nAntenatal Care (ANC): Comprehensive healthcare surveillance from conception until labor. Objectives: monitor maternal-fetal health;\r\ndetect and manage high-risk obstetrical conditions early (pre-eclampsia, gestational diabetes, severe anemia, Rh isoimmunization);\r\nprovide prophylaxis (iron-folate, calcium, Td/Tdap immunization); birth preparedness and complication readiness.\r\nPostnatal Care (PNC): Specialized medical care provided during the 6-week puerperium (42 days post-delivery). Importance: early\r\ndetection and management of postpartum hemorrhage (PPH), puerperal sepsis, venous thromboembolism, and postpartum depression;\r\nestablish and support exclusive breastfeeding; neonatal surveillance (jaundice, cord care, immunization); postpartum contraception\r\ncounseling.\r\nQ 28 Stages and Physiological Changes of Labor\r\nThe Four Stages of Labor:\r\n1st Stage (Cervical Dilatation): Onset of regular uterine contractions to full cervical dilatation (10 cm). Divided into Latent phase\r\n(slow dilatation up to 4–5 cm) and Active phase (rapid dilatation ≥1 cm/h).\r\n2nd Stage (Fetal Expulsion): Full dilatation (10 cm) to complete delivery of the fetus. Propelled by Cardinal Movements:\r\nEngagement, Descent, Flexion, Internal rotation, Extension, Restitution, External rotation, and Expulsion.\r\n3rd Stage (Placental): Complete delivery of baby to complete delivery of placenta and membranes (normal ≤30 min). Managed\r\nwith AMTSL: Oxytocin (10 IU IM), controlled cord traction, and fundal massage.\r\n4th Stage (Observation): Immediate 1–2 hours postpartum for monitoring maternal hemodynamics, uterine contraction tone, and\r\nvaginal bleeding.\r\nMajor Physiological Changes During Labor: Uterine muscular action shows fundal dominance with rhythmic contraction (transient\r\nshortening) and retraction (permanent shortening without relaxation), creating a thick upper active segment and thin lower passive\r\nsegment separated by the physiological retraction ring; Cervical effacement (shortening/thinning) and dilatation driven by hydrostatic bag\r\nof waters and fetal head pressure; Hemodynamic \"autotransfusion\" of 300–500 mL blood into maternal circulation during each\r\ncontraction increasing cardiac output and blood pressure; Hyperventilation causing respiratory alkalosis followed by metabolic lactic\r\nacidosis.\r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\nHIGH-YIELD MEDICAL REVISION (05-MARK QUESTIONS) QUESTIONS 01 – 32 MASTER COMPREHENSIVE BANK\r\nMedical & Allied Health Sciences Examination Series • 5-Mark Long-Form Outlines Page 10 of 12\r\nQ 29 Menopause\r\nDefinition & Physiology: Permanent cessation of menses resulting from loss of ovarian follicular activity, clinically diagnosed\r\nretrospectively after 12 consecutive months of amenorrhea (mean age 51y). Ovarian depletion of follicles causes circulating estradiol\r\n(E2) to fall, removing hypothalamic-pituitary negative feedback and producing hypergonadotropism with marked elevation of FSH (>30\r\n40 IU/L) and LH.\r\nClinical Manifestations:\r\nVasomotor: Hot flashes (intense sudden heat, flushing, sweating, chills from hypothalamic thermoregulatory instability), night sweats,\r\nsleep disturbances.\r\nGenitourinary Syndrome of Menopause (GSM): Vaginal epithelial atrophy, dryness, dyspareunia, pruritus, loss of rugae, elevated\r\npH >5.0, urinary frequency/urgency, and recurrent UTIs.\r\nPsychological & Physical: Irritability, mood swings, depression, forgetfulness, loss of libido, breast atrophy, skin collagen loss, and\r\narthralgias.\r\nComplications: Accelerated osteoporosis and fragility fractures (trabecular bone loss from osteoclast disinhibition); Cardiovascular\r\ndisease (atherogenic lipid shifts: elevated LDL, decreased HDL; loss of estrogen vascular protection).\r\nManagement: Lifestyle: weight-bearing exercise, calcium (1,200 mg/day), vitamin D (800–1,000 IU/day), trigger avoidance; Hormone\r\nReplacement Therapy (HRT): Estrogen-only for hysterectomized women; Combined Estrogen-Progestin for women with an intact uterus\r\n(progestin prevents endometrial cancer); Non-hormonal alternatives: SSRIs/SNRIs (Venlafaxine), Gabapentin, Fezolinetant (NK3\r\nreceptor antagonist); Topical vaginal estrogen creams for GSM.\r\nQ 30 Common Gynecological Infections & Uterine/Vaginal Disorders\r\nCommon Gynecological Infections:\r\nBacterial Vaginosis: Dysbiosis with loss of Lactobacillus and overgrowth of anaerobes (G. vaginalis). Thin grayish-white discharge\r\nwith \"fishy\" odor. Diagnosis by Amsel's criteria: pH >4.5, positive Whiff test (KOH), Clue cells on wet mount. Rx: oral/topical\r\nMetronidazole.\r\nVulvovaginal Candidiasis:C. albicans. Intense vulvovaginal pruritus, erythema, thick white curd-like \"cottage cheese\" discharge,\r\nnormal pH (<4.5), pseudohyphae on 10% KOH. Rx: single-dose oral Fluconazole (150 mg) or Clotrimazole pessary.\r\nTrichomoniasis:T. vaginalis (flagellated protozoan, STD). Frothy greenish-yellow malodorous discharge, severe pruritus, \r\n\"strawberry cervix\". Rx: oral Metronidazole; treat partner.\r\nPelvic Inflammatory Disease (PID): Ascending infection (Chlamydia, Gonorrhea). Bilateral pelvic pain, high fever, purulent\r\ndischarge, marked cervical motion tenderness (\"chandelier sign\"). Rx: IM Ceftriaxone plus oral Doxycycline plus oral\r\nMetronidazole for 14 days.\r\nUterine & Vaginal Disorders:\r\nUterine Fibroids (Leiomyomas): Benign smooth muscle tumors. Menorrhagia, dysmenorrhea, pelvic pressure, bulk symptoms. Rx:\r\nTranexamic acid, Levonorgestrel IUD, GnRH agonists, myomectomy, hysterectomy.\r\nEndometriosis & Adenomyosis: Ectopic endometrial tissue outside uterus or in myometrium. Severe dysmenorrhea, chronic pelvic\r\npain, deep dyspareunia, subfertility. Rx: Progestins, OCPs, laparoscopic ablation.\r\nPelvic Organ Prolapse: Vaginal bulge, pelvic heaviness, cystocele/rectocele. Rx: Kegel exercises, supportive pessaries, pelvic floor\r\nrepair.\r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\nHIGH-YIELD MEDICAL REVISION (05-MARK QUESTIONS) QUESTIONS 01 – 32 MASTER COMPREHENSIVE BANK\r\nMedical & Allied Health Sciences Examination Series • 5-Mark Long-Form Outlines Page 11 of 12\r\nQ 31 Hysterectomy and Dilatation & Curettage (D&C)\r\nHysterectomy:\r\nDefinition: Surgical removal of the uterus (Total: body + cervix; Subtotal: body only; Radical: uterus, cervix, parametrium, upper\r\nvagina, lymph nodes). Performed via abdominal, vaginal, or laparoscopic routes.\r\nIndications: Symptomatic uterine fibroids failing medical therapy, refractory abnormal uterine bleeding (AUB), gynecological\r\nmalignancies (endometrial/cervical cancer), severe adenomyosis/endometriosis, advanced uterovaginal prolapse, emergency\r\nintractable postpartum hemorrhage.\r\nComplications: Hemorrhage, ureteric injury (proximity to uterine arteries), bladder/bowel injury, pelvic hematoma/abscess, surgical\r\nsite infection, DVT/PE, vaginal vault prolapse, and psychosexual distress.\r\nDilatation and Curettage (D&C):\r\nDefinition: Minor operative procedure involving progressive mechanical dilatation of the cervical canal followed by scraping and\r\nevacuation of endometrial tissue with a curette.\r\nIndications: Diagnostic: evaluation of postmenopausal bleeding, abnormal uterine bleeding in women >40y, suspected endometrial\r\nhyperplasia/carcinoma; Therapeutic: evacuation of retained products of conception (incomplete/missed miscarriage), molar\r\npregnancy evacuation, temporary control of acute refractory uterine bleeding, polyp removal.\r\nComplications:Uterine perforation (risk of bowel/vessel injury), cervical laceration/incompetence, acute endometritis/salpingitis, \r\nAsherman's syndrome (intrauterine synechiae from over-vigorous curettage causing amenorrhea/infertility), and hemorrhage.\r\nQ 32 Urinary Incontinence in Women\r\nDefinition: Involuntary, objectively demonstrable loss or leakage of urine causing physical hygiene, social, or psychological distress.\r\nTypes & Causes:\r\nStress Incontinence (SUI, ~50%): Leakage on effort, exertion, coughing, sneezing, or laughing without detrusor contraction. Caused\r\nby pelvic floor muscle laxity, urethral hypermobility, and intrinsic sphincteric deficiency (risk factors: multiparity, vaginal deliveries,\r\npostmenopausal estrogen deficiency, obesity, chronic cough).\r\nUrge Incontinence (UUI / Overactive Bladder, ~20%): Involuntary leakage preceded by an intense, sudden, uncontrollable urge to\r\nvoid. Caused by detrusor overactivity (idiopathic, stroke, Parkinson's, MS, UTI, bladder stones).\r\nMixed Incontinence (~30%): Features of both stress and urge incontinence.\r\nOverflow: Involuntary dribbling from a chronically overfilled, distended bladder due to bladder outlet obstruction (pelvic prolapse) or\r\nhypotonic detrusor (diabetic neuropathy).\r\nFunctional: Inability to reach toilet due to severe physical or cognitive disability (dementia, severe arthritis).\r\nClinical Features: Leakage on strain/cough (SUI); urinary urgency, daytime frequency (>8 times), and nocturia (UUI); continuous\r\ndribbling (overflow); perineal skin dermatitis, secondary UTIs, anxiety, and social withdrawal.\r\nManagement:\r\nConservative & Behavioral (First-line): Lifestyle: weight loss, fluid moderation, avoid caffeine/alcohol; Pelvic Floor Muscle\r\nTraining (Kegel exercises): at least 3 sets of 8–12 maximal contractions daily for ≥3 months (first-line for SUI); Bladder training\r\n(timed voiding) for UUI.\r\nPharmacological: For UUI: Antimuscarinics (Oxybutynin, Tolterodine, Solifenacin) or β3-agonist (Mirabegron); Local topical vaginal\r\nestrogen for postmenopausal women; SUI: Duloxetine (SNRI).\r\nInterventional & Surgical: Intravesical OnabotulinumtoxinA injections or sacral neuromodulation for refractory UUI; Mid-Urethral\r\nSling (MUS: TVT / TOT) synthetic mesh placement (gold standard for refractory SUI); Burch colposuspension; Periurethral bulking\r\nagents.\r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\nHIGH-YIELD MEDICAL REVISION (05-MARK QUESTIONS) QUESTIONS 01 – 32 MASTER COMPREHENSIVE BANK\r\nMedical & Allied Health Sciences Examination Series • 5-Mark Long-Form Outlines Page 12 of 12\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-06T18:50:36+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>"}
{"step_index":10,"source":"SYSTEM","type":"CHECKPOINT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-06T13:20:36Z","content":"{{ CHECKPOINT 1 }}\n **The earlier parts of this conversation have been truncated due to its long length. The following content summarizes the truncated context so that you may continue your work. **\n\n\n# User Requests\nThe following were user requests from the truncated conversation in chronological order:\n1. You are a highly precise text formatting assistant. Your task is to reformat any medical or academic text I provide into a highly condensed, punctuation-free, continuous block of text to save space, while preserving the logical flow of information.\r\n\r\nStrict Formatting Rules:\r\n\r\nStructure: The output MUST be a single continuous block of text with single spacing.\r\n\r\nHeadings: Make all main topics and subtopics bold (e.g., Main Topic).\r\n\r\nLists & Numbers: Keep all numbers (1, 2, 3...) and bullet points (•) inline within the text block. Do not remove them; they must remain in the natural flow of the text so the answers still make sense.\r\n\r\nDividers: Use exactly three dashes (---) as a divider to separate main topics.\r\n\r\nSpecial Characters: If there is a sequence of forward slashes (e.g., /////////////), you MUST NOT remove them. Keep them exactly where they appear in the source text.\r\n\r\nNegative Prompting (WHAT NOT TO DO):\r\n\r\nNO PUNCTUATION: DO NOT use any standard punctuation. Remove all periods, commas, c\n<truncated 1973 bytes>\n2. ///Gangrene — \r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\nGangrene is the death and decomposition of body tissue caused by loss of blood supply, infection, or a combination of both. It commonly affects the extremities but may occur in other organs.\r\n\r\nEtiology / Causes\r\n\r\n1. Reduced blood supply\r\n    * Atherosclerosis\r\n    * Peripheral arterial disease\r\n    * Thrombosis or embolism\r\n2. Infection\r\n    * Severe bacterial infection\r\n3. Trauma\r\n    * Crush injuries\r\n    * Severe burns\r\n4. Diabetes mellitus\r\n5. Pressure and tissue ischemia\r\n6. Frostbite\r\n\r\nTypes of Gangrene\r\n\r\n1. Dry gangrene\r\n\r\n* Usually caused by progressive loss of arterial blood supply.\r\n* Tissue becomes dry, shriveled and black.\r\n* Usually develops slowly.\r\n* There is a clear line of demarcation between healthy and dead tissue.\r\n* Infection is initially minimal.\r\n\r\n2. Wet gangrene\r\n\r\n* Occurs when ischemic tissue becomes infected.\r\n* Tissue is swollen, soft and discolored.\r\n* Pus and foul-smelling discharge may be present.\r\n* Spreads rapidly.\r\n* Can lead to sepsi\n<truncated 2504 bytes>\n3. Medical & Allied Health Sciences: High-Yield Revision\r\nComprehensive 45-Question Master Bank (02-Mark Definitions, Features & Classifications)\r\n45 EXAM QUESTIONS 2-MARK STANDARD FORMAT RESPIRATORY • CVS • ICU • DERMATOLOGY • OB-GYN\r\nPART I: RESPIRATORY PATHOLOGY & PULMONARY DISORDERS\r\n01 Bronchial Asthma\r\nDefinition:A chronic inflammatory disorder of the airways characterized by bronchial hyperresponsiveness, chronic\r\ninflammation, and recurrent episodes of reversible airflow obstruction and bronchospasm.\r\nCommon Triggers:Inhaled allergens (pollen, dust mites, animal dander), viral respiratory tract infections, cold ambient air,\r\nphysical exertion, atmospheric smoke/pollutants, and medications (aspirin, non-selective beta-blockers).\r\n02 Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD)\r\nDefinition:A common, preventable, and treatable pulmonary disease characterized by persistent, usually progressive airflow\r\nlimitation caused by a combination of small airway disease (obstructive bronchiolitis) and paren\n<truncated 25177 bytes>\n4. HIGH YIELD MEDICAL REVISION 2 MARK QUESTIONS QUESTIONS 01 45 RAPID REVISION GUIDE Medical Allied Health Sciences Examination Series Page 8 of 8  remove this for all the paages not this one only remove it and give exact aobe one \n5. Medical & Allied Health Sciences: Master Examination Guide\r\nComprehensive 32-Question Master Bank (05-Mark Long-Form Model Answers)\r\nQUESTIONS 01 – 32 5-MARK EXAMINATION FORMAT SYSTEMIC PATHOLOGY • CRITICAL CARE • DERMATOLOGY • OB-GYN\r\nPART I: RESPIRATORY PATHOLOGY & PULMONARY MEDICINE (Q01 – Q07)\r\nQ 01 Acute Bronchitis\r\nDefinition: An acute, self-limiting inflammation of the tracheobronchial mucosa presenting characteristically with persistent cough with or\r\nwithout sputum production.\r\nEtiology:\r\nViral (>90%): Influenza A/B, Parainfluenza, RSV, Rhinovirus, Adenovirus, Coronavirus.\r\nBacterial (<10%):Mycoplasma pneumoniae, Chlamydophila pneumoniae, Bordetella pertussis.\r\nNon-Infectious: Chemical fumes, dusts, tobacco smoke, air pollutants.\r\nClinical Features: Persistent cough (initially dry/hacking, later mucoid or purulent, lasting 1–3 weeks); retrosternal burning soreness on\r\ncoughing; low-grade fever, malaise, myalgias; scattered expiratory wheezes/rhonchi and coarse crackles that clear upon \n<truncated 51278 bytes>\n\n# Previous Session Summary:\nHere is the continuation summary for the next agent:\n\n### 1. Outstanding User Requests\n- **Format the \"32-Question Master Bank\" text block.** (Status: IMPLEMENTATION)\n  - The user has provided a new, large block of medical text (\"Medical & Allied Health Sciences: Master Examination Guide... QUESTIONS 01 – 32\"). The agent must format this text according to the highly strict rules established in previous turns, and output ONLY the formatted text.\n  - *Crucial context from prior turns:* The user explicitly requested the removal of repeating PDF pagination/footer artifacts (e.g., \"Page 1 of 12\", \"HIGH-YIELD MEDICAL REVISION...\"). These must be silently excised from the new text.\n\n### 2. User Knowledge\n- **Strict Formatting Rules (Quote from User):**\n  - \"Structure: The output MUST be a single continuous block of text with single spacing.\" (No line breaks/carriage returns anywhere).\n  - \"Headings: Make all main topics and subtopics bold (e.g., Ma