{"step_index":9,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-06T13:25:07Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nMedical & Allied Health Sciences: Master Examination Guide\r\nComprehensive 32-Question Master Bank (05-Mark Long-Form Model Answers)\r\nQUESTIONS 01 – 32 5-MARK EXAMINATION FORMAT SYSTEMIC PATHOLOGY • CRITICAL CARE • DERMATOLOGY • OB-GYN\r\nPART I: RESPIRATORY PATHOLOGY & PULMONARY MEDICINE (Q01 – Q07)\r\nQ 01 Acute Bronchitis\r\nDefinition: An acute, self-limiting inflammation of the tracheobronchial mucosa presenting characteristically with persistent cough with or\r\nwithout sputum production.\r\nEtiology:\r\nViral (>90%): Influenza A/B, Parainfluenza, RSV, Rhinovirus, Adenovirus, Coronavirus.\r\nBacterial (<10%):Mycoplasma pneumoniae, Chlamydophila pneumoniae, Bordetella pertussis.\r\nNon-Infectious: Chemical fumes, dusts, tobacco smoke, air pollutants.\r\nClinical Features: Persistent cough (initially dry/hacking, later mucoid or purulent, lasting 1–3 weeks); retrosternal burning soreness on\r\ncoughing; low-grade fever, malaise, myalgias; scattered expiratory wheezes/rhonchi and coarse crackles that clear upon coughing.\r\nInvestigations: Primarily clinical diagnosis. Chest X-ray (PA view) is usually normal; indicated to exclude pneumonia if tachypnea,\r\ntachycardia, high fever, or consolidation signs are present. Sputum PCR only for suspected B. pertussis.\r\nComplications: Secondary bronchopneumonia, acute asthma/COPD exacerbation, chronic post-infectious bronchial\r\nhyperresponsiveness (>4 weeks).\r\nManagement: Supportive hydration and steam inhalation; Paracetamol/Ibuprofen for chest soreness and fever; Dextromethorphan for\r\ndisruptive dry cough; inhaled Salbutamol for bronchospasm/wheeze. Routine antibiotics are not recommended unless elderly with\r\ncomorbidities or confirmed pertussis (Azithromycin).\r\nQ 02 Bronchial Pneumonia (Bronchopneumonia)\r\nDefinition: Acute suppurative inflammation of the pulmonary parenchyma centered around terminal and respiratory bronchioles,\r\npresenting with patchy, multifocal consolidation involving multiple pulmonary lobes.\r\nCauses: Bacterial: Streptococcus pneumoniae, Staphylococcus aureus, Klebsiella pneumoniae, Pseudomonas aeruginosa, H.\r\ninfluenzae. Predisposing factors include extremes of age, aspiration, malnutrition, post-viral airway injury, and immobilization.\r\nClinical Presentation: High-grade fever with chills/rigors, productive cough with purulent/rust-colored sputum, progressive dyspnea,\r\npleuritic chest pain; tachypnea, localized dullness on percussion, bronchial breath sounds, increased vocal resonance, and coarse\r\ninspiratory crackles.\r\nInvestigations: Chest X-ray (shows bilateral patchy, mottled opacities centered around bronchi); Sputum Gram stain and culture/\r\nsensitivity; blood cultures; CBC (leukocytosis with left shift, elevated CRP/ESR); ABG for hypoxemia.\r\nComplications: Parapneumonic effusion, empyema thoracis, lung abscess formation, sepsis/septic shock, Type 1 respiratory failure,\r\nand ARDS.\r\nTreatment: Supplemental O2 (target SpO2 ≥92%); IV fluids and chest physiotherapy; Empiric antibiotics: IV Ceftriaxone or Amoxicillin\r\nclavulanate plus Azithromycin (community-acquired); Piperacillin-tazobactam or Meropenem plus Vancomycin for hospital-acquired/\r\nMRSA; tailored to culture sensitivities for 7–10 days.\r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\nHIGH-YIELD MEDICAL REVISION (05-MARK QUESTIONS) QUESTIONS 01 – 32 MASTER COMPREHENSIVE BANK\r\nMedical & Allied Health Sciences Examination Series • 5-Mark Long-Form Outlines Page 1 of 12\r\nQ 03 Pleural Effusion\r\nDefinition: Pathological accumulation of excess fluid within the pleural cavity (>15 mL) resulting from an imbalance between fluid\r\nformation and absorption.\r\nTypes & Causes:\r\nTransudative (Altered Starling forces): Congestive heart failure, liver cirrhosis with ascites, nephrotic syndrome, severe\r\nhypoalbuminemia.\r\nExudative (Inflammatory/vascular permeability): Parapneumonic infection, tuberculosis, bronchogenic/metastatic carcinoma,\r\npulmonary infarction/PE, autoimmune diseases (SLE, RA).\r\nClinical Features: Progressive exertional dyspnea, pleuritic chest pain, dry cough; decreased chest expansion on affected side,\r\ncontralateral mediastinal shift (massive effusion), stony dull percussion note, absent/diminished breath sounds and vocal resonance.\r\nInvestigations: Chest X-ray (costophrenic angle blunting >175 mL, homogenous opacity with lateral upward-curving meniscus sign);\r\nThoracic Ultrasound; Diagnostic thoracocentesis using Light's Criteria for exudates (Fluid/Serum Protein >0.5, Fluid/Serum LDH >0.6,\r\nor Fluid LDH >2/3 upper limit of serum LDH); ADA (>40 U/L indicates TB), cytology, and microbiology.\r\nManagement: Treat etiology (diuretics for CHF; ATT for TB; chemotherapy for malignancy); Therapeutic thoracocentesis (drain ≤1,000\r\n1,500 mL/session); Intercostal chest drain (ICD) for empyema (pH <7.20) or hemothorax; Chemical pleurodesis (talc, bleomycin) for\r\nrecurrent malignant effusions.\r\nQ 04 Pneumothorax\r\nDefinition: Accumulation of free air within the pleural space between the parietal and visceral pleura, causing partial or complete lung\r\ncollapse.\r\nTypes & Causes:\r\nSpontaneous: Primary (rupture of subpleural apical blebs in tall, young men); Secondary (COPD bullae, cavitary TB, cystic fibrosis).\r\nTraumatic / Iatrogenic: Penetrating/blunt chest trauma, rib fractures, subclavian line placement, pleural biopsy, mechanical\r\nbarotrauma.\r\nTension Pneumothorax: One-way valve mechanism allowing air entry during inspiration but trapping air on expiration, generating\r\nmassive intrapleural pressure.\r\nClinical Features: Sudden unilateral sharp pleuritic chest pain and dyspnea; decreased expansion, hyperresonant percussion note,\r\nabsent/diminished breath sounds. Tension hallmark: cyanosis, hypotension, tachycardia, tracheal deviation to the contralateral side, and\r\ndistended neck veins.\r\nComplications: Obstructive shock, severe hypoxemic respiratory failure, hemopneumothorax, re-expansion pulmonary edema.\r\nEmergency Management:\r\nTension Pneumothorax: Immediate emergency needle decompression (14–16G cannula in 2nd ICS midclavicular line or 4th/5th ICS\r\nanterior axillary line) without awaiting X-ray.\r\nDefinitive Tube Thoracostomy: Intercostal chest tube (ICD) with underwater seal inserted in the safe triangle.\r\nHigh-flow supplemental oxygen (accelerates pleural air resorption); VATS pleurodesis for persistent leaks (>48h) or recurrence.\r\nQ 05 Lung Abscess\r\nDefinition: Localized parenchymal microbial necrosis and cavity formation containing purulent necrotic debris and fluid.\r\nEtiology: Aspiration of oral/gastric secretions containing anaerobes (Peptostreptococcus, Bacteroides, Fusobacterium) in patients with\r\nimpaired consciousness (alcoholism, stroke, epilepsy) or poor dentition; Necrotizing pneumonia (S. aureus, Klebsiella); Bronchial\r\nobstruction by carcinoma; Septic emboli (tricuspid endocarditis).\r\nClinical Manifestations: High swinging fever with rigors and night sweats, malaise, progressive weight loss; cough productive of\r\ncopious, foul-smelling, putrid purulent sputum; hemoptysis; pallor, rapid finger clubbing, dullness to percussion, and cavernous/amphoric\r\nbreath sounds over cavity.\r\nInvestigations: Chest X-ray (thick-walled solitary cavity with a prominent air-fluid level surrounded by consolidation); CECT Chest\r\n(delineates cavity and excludes empyema); Sputum microbiology (Gram, AFB, culture); Bronchoscopy to exclude obstructing\r\nendobronchial neoplasm.\r\nComplications: Empyema/pyopneumothorax, bronchopleural fistula, massive fatal hemoptysis, metastatic brain abscess, secondary\r\namyloidosis.\r\nTreatment: Prolonged antimicrobial therapy (4–8 weeks): IV Ampicillin-sulbactam, Amoxicillin-clavulanate, or Clindamycin, transitioned\r\nto oral; Postural drainage and chest physiotherapy; Percutaneous catheter drainage for refractory cavities >6 cm; Surgical lobectomy for\r\nmassive hemoptysis or suspected underlying malignancy.\r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\nHIGH-YIELD MEDICAL REVISION (05-MARK QUESTIONS) QUESTIONS 01 – 32 MASTER COMPREHENSIVE BANK\r\nMedical & Allied Health Sciences Examination Series • 5-Mark Long-Form Outlines Page 2 of 12\r\nQ 06 Respiratory Failure\r\nDefinition: Inability of the respiratory system to maintain adequate gas exchange, defined as arterial PaO2 <60 mmHg with or without\r\nPaCO2 >50 mmHg on ambient air at sea level.\r\nTypes & Causes:\r\nType 1 (Hypoxemic): PaO2 <60 mmHg with normal/low PaCO2. Caused by V/Q mismatch and right-to-left shunting: ARDS, severe\r\npneumonia, acute pulmonary edema, massive PE, diffuse interstitial fibrosis.\r\nType 2 (Hypercapnic): PaCO2 >50 mmHg with companion hypoxemia. Caused by alveolar hypoventilation/pump failure: severe\r\nCOPD, acute severe asthma, opioid overdose, myasthenia gravis, Guillain-Barré, severe kyphoscoliosis.\r\nClinical Features: Hypoxemia: dyspnea, tachypnea, tachycardia, central cyanosis, restlessness, confusion. Hypercapnia: bounding\r\npulses, warm flushed hands, morning headache, asterixis (flapping tremor), somnolence, and CO2 narcosis/coma.\r\nInvestigations: Arterial Blood Gas (ABG) analysis (confirms diagnosis, defines acidosis/alkalosis and compensation); Continuous pulse\r\noximetry; Chest X-ray and CT thorax; Spirometry.\r\nManagement: Controlled oxygen therapy (target SpO2 94–98% in Type 1; strictly 88–92% using a Venturi mask in Type 2 COPD to\r\npreserve hypoxic drive); Non-Invasive Ventilation (BiPAP) for hypercapnic COPD exacerbations; Invasive mechanical ventilation if severe\r\nacidosis (pH <7.25), exhaustion, or coma; Bronchodilators, systemic steroids, diuretics, and antidotes (Naloxone).\r\nQ 07 Occupational Lung Diseases\r\nDefinition: Preventable non-neoplastic and neoplastic disorders caused by repeated inhalation of mineral dusts, organic particles,\r\nfumes, or toxic gases in the work environment.\r\nCauses & Common Types:\r\nSilicosis: Inhalation of crystalline SiO2 in stone cutting, sandblasting, quarrying, foundry work; produces fibrotic silicotic nodules and\r\nhigh risk of tuberculosis (silicotuberculosis).\r\nAsbestosis: Inhalation of asbestos fibers in shipbuilding, demolition, roofing; causes bibasilar interstitial fibrosis, calcified pleural\r\nplaques, and malignant mesothelioma.\r\nCoal Workers' Pneumoconiosis (CWP): Coal dust inhalation leading to anthracosis and progressive massive fibrosis.\r\nOrganic: Byssinosis (cotton dust, \"Monday chest tightness\"), Farmer's lung (thermophilic actinomycetes in moldy hay).\r\nClinical Features: Insidious exertional dyspnea, chronic dry or productive cough (melanoptysis in CWP), wheezing, bibasilar end\r\ninspiratory crackles, reduced chest expansion, digital clubbing, and late cor pulmonale.\r\nPrevention: Engineering controls (wet drilling, local exhaust ventilation, toxic material substitution); Administrative controls (worker\r\nrotation); PPE (certified N95/HEPA particulate respirators); Periodic medical screening with spirometry and chest X-rays (ILO\r\nclassification).\r\nManagement: Immediate permanent removal from dust exposure; Bronchodilators and supplemental O2; Pneumococcal and influenza\r\nvaccination; Tuberculosis screening; Pulmonary rehabilitation; Medico-legal compensation.\r\nPART II: CARDIOVASCULAR PATHOLOGY & HEMODYNAMICS (Q08 – Q15)\r\nQ 08 Atherosclerosis\r\nDefinition: Chronic inflammatory disease of large/medium muscular and elastic arteries characterized by intimal fibrofatty plaques\r\n(atheromas) narrowing the lumen and weakening the arterial wall.\r\nMajor Risk Factors: Modifiable: Dyslipidemia (high LDL, low HDL), systemic hypertension, cigarette smoking, diabetes mellitus, visceral\r\nobesity, sedentary life. Non-modifiable: Advancing age, male sex, family history of premature CAD.\r\nClinical Manifestations: Coronary: stable angina, acute myocardial infarction; Cerebral: TIAs, ischemic stroke; Peripheral: intermittent\r\nclaudication, critical limb ischemia; Renal: renovascular hypertension.\r\nComplications: Acute plaque rupture with occlusive luminal thrombosis, aneurysmal dilatation/rupture (abdominal aorta),\r\natheroembolism, and progressive ischemic cardiomyopathy.\r\nInvestigations: Fasting lipid profile, HbA1c, hs-CRP; ECG and treadmill exercise test (TMT); Carotid Doppler (intima-media thickness)\r\nand Ankle-Brachial Index (ABI <0.9); Coronary CT angiography and conventional catheter angiography.\r\nManagement: Lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, aerobic exercise, smoking cessation; Pharmacotherapy: High-intensity statins (Atorvastatin\r\n40–80 mg to achieve LDL <55 mg/dL), low-dose Aspirin (75–100 mg), ACE inhibitors, tight glycemic/BP control; Revascularization: PCI\r\nwith drug-eluting stents, CABG, or endarterectomy.\r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\nHIGH-YIELD MEDICAL REVISION (05-MARK QUESTIONS) QUESTIONS 01 – 32 MASTER COMPREHENSIVE BANK\r\nMedical & Allied Health Sciences Examination Series • 5-Mark Long-Form Outlines Page 3 of 12\r\nQ 09 Thrombosis and Embolism\r\nDefinitions: Thrombosis is the intravascular formation of a solid/semisolid mass of fibrin, platelets, and cells in intact unruptured vessels\r\nduring life. Embolism is the mechanical occlusion of a vessel by a detached intravascular mass transported away from its origin.\r\nCauses (Virchow's Triad): 1. Endothelial injury (atherosclerosis, trauma, vasculitis); 2. Altered blood flow (venous stasis from\r\nimmobility, turbulence from aneurysms); 3. Hypercoagulability (Factor V Leiden, malignancy, antiphospholipid syndrome, oral\r\ncontraceptives). Emboli arise from dislodged DVTs, cardiac mural thrombi (atrial fibrillation), fat (fractures), air, or septic vegetations.\r\nClinical Features: DVT: Unilateral calf swelling, calf pain, erythema, tenderness, positive Homans sign; Pulmonary Embolism: acute\r\ndyspnea, pleuritic chest pain, tachypnea, tachycardia, hemoptysis, syncope; Acute Arterial Embolism (6 Ps): Pain, Pallor, Pulselessness,\r\nParesthesia, Paralysis, Poikilothermia.\r\nComplications: Fatal massive PE with obstructive shock, ischemic stroke, acute mesenteric ischemia, post-thrombotic syndrome.\r\nDiagnosis: Compression Duplex ultrasound (DVT); quantitative D-dimer assay; CT Pulmonary Angiography (CTPA, gold standard for\r\nPE); Arterial Doppler and CT angiography.\r\nManagement: Anticoagulation: LMWH (Enoxaparin 1 mg/kg SC q12h) or unfractionated heparin, transitioned to DOACs (Apixaban,\r\nRivaroxaban) or Warfarin (target INR 2.0–3.0) for 3–6 months; Thrombolysis (r-tPA / Alteplase) for massive PE with shock or acute\r\nischemic stroke <4.5h; Catheter thrombectomy, surgical embolectomy, and IVC filter placement.\r\nQ 10 Ischemic Heart Disease (IHD)\r\nDefinition: Cardiac syndromes resulting from an imbalance between myocardial oxygen supply and demand, predominantly caused by\r\nfixed atherosclerotic narrowing of epicardial coronary arteries.\r\nRisk Factors: Dyslipidemia, hypertension, cigarette smoking, diabetes mellitus, visceral obesity, metabolic syndrome, male sex, older\r\nage, family history.\r\nClinical Features: Stable angina: retrosternal tightness/pressure radiating to left arm/neck/jaw, provoked by exertion, relieved by rest or\r\nsublingual nitroglycerin within 2–5 min; Acute Coronary Syndromes (NSTEMI/STEMI): severe crushing retrosternal pain lasting >20 min,\r\noccurring at rest, accompanied by diaphoresis, nausea, dyspnea, and syncope.\r\nInvestigations: 12-lead ECG (ST elevation in STEMI, ST depression/T inversion in ischemia/NSTEMI); Cardiac biomarkers (hs-cTnI, hs\r\ncTnT, CK-MB); Echocardiography (regional wall motion abnormalities, LVEF); Coronary angiography (gold standard).\r\nComplications: Malignant ventricular arrhythmias (VT/VF), sudden cardiac death, cardiogenic shock, congestive heart failure, papillary\r\nmuscle rupture with acute mitral regurgitation, ventricular free-wall/septal rupture.\r\nTreatment: Acute ACS: Oxygen (if SpO2 <90%), sublingual Nitroglycerin, chewable Aspirin (300 mg) plus P2Y12 inhibitor (Ticagrelor\r\n180 mg or Clopidogrel 600 mg), Enoxaparin; Primary PCI (<90 min door-to-balloon) or thrombolysis (<120 min); Long-term: dual\r\nantiplatelet therapy, high-intensity statin, cardioselective beta-blocker, and ACE inhibitor.\r\nQ 11 Cardiac Failure (Heart Failure)\r\nDefinition: Complex clinical syndrome resulting from structural or functional impairment of ventricular filling or blood ejection, leaving the\r\nheart unable to meet the metabolic demands of tissues at normal venous pressures.\r\nTypes & Causes:\r\nTypes: HFrEF (systolic, LVEF ≤40%); HFpEF (diastolic, LVEF ≥50%); Left-sided vs. Right-sided; Acute vs. Chronic.\r\nCauses: Coronary artery disease and myocardial infarction (most common), chronic hypertension, valvular heart disease, dilated\r\ncardiomyopathies, cor pulmonale.\r\nClinical Manifestations: Left-sided (Pulmonary congestion): exertional dyspnea, orthopnea, paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea (PND),\r\nbibasilar crackles, S3 gallop; Right-sided (Systemic venous congestion): elevated JVP, bilateral pitting pedal edema, congestive\r\nhepatomegaly, ascites.\r\nComplications: Acute pulmonary edema, ventricular arrhythmias, cardiorenal syndrome, cardiac cirrhosis, mural thrombi with systemic\r\nembolism.\r\nManagement: Lifestyle: dietary sodium (<2 g/day) and fluid restriction, weight monitoring; Guideline-Directed Medical Therapy (GDMT \r\n4 Pillars for HFrEF): 1. ARNI (Sacubitril-valsartan) or ACEi/ARB; 2. Beta-blocker (Carvedilol, Bisoprolol, Metoprolol succinate); 3. MRA\r\n(Spironolactone); 4. SGLT2 inhibitor (Dapagliflozin/Empagliflozin); Diuretics (Furosemide) for volume overload; Device therapy: ICD and\r\nCRT-D; LVAD or heart transplantation.\r\n• \r\n• \r\nHIGH-YIELD MEDICAL REVISION (05-MARK QUESTIONS) QUESTIONS 01 – 32 MASTER COMPREHENSIVE BANK\r\nMedical & Allied Health Sciences Examination Series • 5-Mark Long-Form Outlines Page 4 of 12\r\nQ 12 Rheumatic Heart Disease (RHD)\r\nDefinition: Chronic permanent cardiac valvular damage resulting from single or recurrent episodes of acute rheumatic fever (ARF)\r\ninitiated by untreated Group A β-hemolytic streptococcal (GAS) pharyngitis.\r\nEtiology & Pathogenesis:Streptococcus pyogenes. Driven by molecular mimicry: streptococcal M-protein cross-reacts with human\r\ncardiac myosin and sarcolemma, producing pancarditis with pathognomonic granulomatous Aschoff bodies.\r\nClinical Features: History of ARF (Jones criteria). Valvular involvement (Mitral >90%, then Aortic): \r\nMitral Stenosis: Exertional dyspnea, hemoptysis, malar flush, tapping apex beat, loud S1, opening snap, low-pitched rumbling mid\r\ndiastolic murmur at the apex.\r\nMitral Regurgitation: Holosystolic murmur radiating to axilla; Aortic lesions cause early diastolic decrescendo murmur.\r\nComplications: Congestive heart failure, atrial fibrillation, left atrial mural thrombi with stroke, and infective endocarditis.\r\nInvestigations: Doppler Echocardiography (shows thickened valve leaflets, chordal shortening, \"hockey-stick\" anterior mitral leaflet,\r\npressure gradients); 12-lead ECG (left atrial enlargement / P-mitrale, AF); Chest X-ray (straightening of left heart border, double right\r\ncontour); Elevated ASO titer.\r\nManagement: Secondary penicillin prophylaxis: Benzathine penicillin G (1.2 million units IM q3–4 weeks) for at least 10 years or until\r\nage 40; Medical: diuretics, beta-blockers/digoxin for AF, Warfarin anticoagulation (target INR 2.0–3.0); Surgery/Intervention:\r\nPercutaneous Transvenous Mitral Commissurotomy (PTMC) or surgical prosthetic valve replacement.\r\nQ 13 Cardiomyopathies\r\nDefinition: Heterogeneous group of intrinsic myocardial disorders associated with mechanical/electrical cardiac dysfunction, not\r\nsecondary to coronary disease, hypertension, or valvular disease.\r\nTypes & Causes:\r\nDilated (DCM): Ventricular dilatation with systolic dysfunction (LVEF <40%). Causes: idiopathic, titin mutations, viral myocarditis,\r\nchronic alcoholism, doxorubicin toxicity, peripartum.\r\nHypertrophic (HCM): Asymmetric septal hypertrophy with preserved/hyperdynamic systolic function. Autosomal dominant mutation\r\nin sarcomeric genes (β-MHC, MYBPC3).\r\nRestrictive (RCM): Rigid, non-compliant ventricular walls causing impaired diastolic filling. Infiltrative causes: amyloidosis,\r\nsarcoidosis, hemochromatosis.\r\nARVC: Fibrofatty replacement of RV myocardium predisposing to ventricular arrhythmias.\r\nClinical Manif